---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StatusStrip from '../components/StatusStrip.astro';
import tools from '../data/tools.json';
import status from '../data/status.json';
---
<Base
  title="The engine, and its nine tools"
  description="What has actually been built: nine tools over one engine, what each is for, how they chain, and what each of them refuses to do."
  derivesFrom={[
    { label: 'CODE-STATUS.md', href: 'https://github.com/ief-global/thamizh-mcp-design/blob/main/CODE-STATUS.md' },
    { label: 'the blueprint §6 and §8', href: 'https://github.com/ief-global/thamizh-mcp-design/blob/main/Thamizh-MCP-blueprint.md' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="pagehead">
      <p class="kicker">The scorecard</p>
      <h1>What we have actually built</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Nine tools over one engine, with four ways to reach it. This page is the honest inventory:
        what each tool is for, how they chain together, and what each one refuses to do. The refusals
        are the part worth reading.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="statwrap"><StatusStrip /></div>

    <section>
      <h2>One engine, four front doors</h2>
      <p>
        The analysis lives in a plain Python library. Everything else is a thin adapter on top of it.
        An MCP server so an AI assistant can call it, a REST API for apps, a browser page for people
        who will never install anything, and a command line.
      </p>

      <div class="scroll-x">
        <div class="flowmap" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: four heads sit over one engine; the engine consults anchors first, then evolving sources, and writes results back to a knowledge store with provenance.">
          <div class="col">
            <p class="collab">Heads</p>
            <div class="node head">MCP server<span>AI assistants</span></div>
            <div class="node head">REST API<span>apps</span></div>
            <div class="node head">Web page<span>everyone else</span></div>
            <div class="node head">CLI<span>terminals</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
          <div class="col">
            <p class="collab">One engine</p>
            <div class="node engine">
              normalise
              <span>reject non-Tamil input</span>
            </div>
            <div class="node engine">
              knowledge store
              <span>cache hit, served with its provenance</span>
            </div>
            <div class="node engine">
              decoder and classifier
              <span>the rules, isolated so nothing re-derives them</span>
            </div>
            <div class="node engine gapnode">
              merge, or return a gap
              <span>every path can end in an honest gap</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
          <div class="col">
            <p class="collab">Sources</p>
            <div class="node anchor">
              Anchors
              <span>ThamizhiMorph FST · pinned Tholkappiyam and Nannūl · curated verb paradigms · cited rule tables</span>
            </div>
            <div class="node evolving">
              Evolving
              <span>Tamil Wiktionary for meaning · English Wiktionary for etymology · attested equivalents lists</span>
            </div>
            <div class="node store">
              Write back
              <span>every claim stored with source, tier and retrieval date</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="src">
        The web page was added later and needed <b>zero changes to the engine</b>. That is the
        evidence that "one engine, many heads" was a real design rather than a diagram in a document.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The nine tools</h2>
      <p>
        Grouped by what they are for. <b>Refuses</b> is not a caveat list, it is the product: a tool
        that declines to answer is the only kind a teacher can safely put in front of a student.
      </p>

      {tools.groups.map((g) => (
        <div class="group">
          <h3 class="gname">{g.name}</h3>
          <p class="gblurb">{g.blurb}</p>
          {g.tools.map((t) => (
            <div class="tool">
              <p class="tname"><code>{t.name}</code></p>
              <p class="tobj ta">{t.objective}</p>
              <p class="tref ta"><span class="lab">Refuses</span> {t.refuses}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      ))}
      <p class="src">
        Summarised from the server's own tool descriptions, read {tools.read_on}. Those descriptions
        are a product surface rather than documentation: a model decides whether to call a tool by
        reading them, and we once measured a model calling these tools 0% of the time under a neutral
        prompt. The server was returning correct answers. Nothing was telling the model to ask.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How a single call chains</h2>
      <p>
        Asking <code>analyze_word</code> one question fans out to the six read tools, merges what they
        return, and reports each field with the source that grounded it. The two writers feed the same
        store from the side, which is how coverage grows.
      </p>
      <ol class="chain">
        <li><b>Normalise</b> the input, and reject anything that is not a single Tamil word.</li>
        <li><b>Ask the store.</b> A hit is served with the provenance it was stored with.</li>
        <li><b>Ask the anchors:</b> the FST for morphology, the cited rule tables and the pinned verses for grammar.</li>
        <li><b>Ask the evolving sources</b> only for what anchors cannot supply, chiefly meaning and etymology.</li>
        <li><b>Merge,</b> keeping competing readings rather than adjudicating them.</li>
        <li><b>Write back</b> what was newly learned, tagged with its source and the date.</li>
        <li><b>Return</b> the answer, with a gap wherever nothing could ground a field.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Step 6 is quietly the most important one on this page. Every resolved analysis is captured as
        verified, provenance-tagged data, so the corpus Tamil is missing accumulates as a by-product
        of ordinary use. That is the bridge to <a href="/#stack">the horizon</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Every source declares two independent things</h2>
      <p>
        How good the evidence is, graded A to D, and what we are legally allowed to do with it. They
        are separate questions and collapsing them causes mistakes in both directions: a source can be
        excellent evidence that we may only consult and cite.
      </p>
      <p>
        The grade is stamped onto every citation the server returns and shown in the app, so a reader
        can see that a claim rests on a pinned classical edition rather than on a community word list.
        This came out of finding that one word list we depend on had no stated licence at all, which
        is written up on <a href="/#stack">the sources layer</a> and stays published rather than
        quietly fixed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What is not done</h2>
      <p>Current, from the code side, verified {status.verified_on}:</p>
      <ul class="gaps">{status.not_done.map((n: string) => <li class="ta">{n}</li>)}</ul>
      <p class="note">
        The formation decoder handles {status.figures.formation_decoded} of
        {' '}{status.figures.formation_in_scope} in-scope words in the everyday sweep. The
        remainder are non-finite forms, and they are blocked on a grammar question we have not
        settled rather than on code. <b>We would rather leave them undecoded than name them wrongly.</b>
      </p>
      <p class="onward"><a href="/why">Why any of this is necessary &rarr;</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .pagehead { margin: clamp(40px, 7vh, 72px) 0 0; }
  .pagehead h1 { font-size: clamp(28px, 4.4vw, 44px); line-height: 1.15; margin: 12px 0 0; max-width: 20ch; }
  .lede { font-size: 19px; color: var(--muted); max-width: 60ch; margin: 20px 0 0; }
  .statwrap { margin: 32px 0 0; }
  .src { font-family: var(--ui); font-size: 13px; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 18px; max-width: 68ch; }
  code { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: .92em; }

  .flowmap { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1.15fr auto 1.15fr; gap: 14px;
    align-items: start; margin: 26px 0 0; min-width: 720px; }
  .col { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; }
  .collab { font-family: var(--ui); font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: .14em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted); font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 2px; }
  .node { border: 1px solid var(--line); background: var(--card); padding: 10px 13px;
    font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.35; }
  .node span { display: block; font-family: var(--ui); font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 5px; line-height: 1.4; }
  .node.head { border-left: 4px solid var(--accent2); }
  .node.engine { border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); }
  .node.gapnode { border-left-color: var(--warn); }
  .node.anchor { border-left: 4px solid var(--ok); }
  .node.evolving { border-left: 4px solid var(--ochre); }
  .node.store { border-left: 4px solid var(--muted); }
  .arrow { align-self: center; color: var(--muted); font-size: 20px; padding-top: 20px; }

  .group { margin: 34px 0 0; }
  .gname { font-size: 13px; font-family: var(--ui); letter-spacing: .13em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }
  .gblurb { color: var(--muted); font-size: 15px; margin: 8px 0 0; }
  .tool { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 16px 0 4px; }
  .tname { margin: 0; font-size: 16px; }
  .tname code { color: var(--accent2); font-weight: 600; font-size: 15.5px; }
  .tobj { margin: 9px 0 0; }
  .tref { margin: 10px 0 0; font-size: 15px; color: var(--muted); border-left: 3px solid var(--warn);
    padding-left: 14px; }
  .tref .lab { font-family: var(--ui); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: .13em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--warn); font-weight: 600; margin-right: 8px; }

  .chain { margin: 20px 0 0; padding-left: 22px; max-width: var(--measure); }
  .chain li { margin: 9px 0; }
  .gaps { margin: 16px 0 0; padding-left: 20px; max-width: var(--measure); color: var(--muted); }
  .gaps li { margin: 7px 0; }
  .onward { font-family: var(--ui); margin-top: 30px; }
</style>
